<BaseLayout title="TerrOps — Julian Nicholls">
  <article class="case shell">
    <header class="case-hero terrops-hero"><div class="eyebrow">Environmental intelligence / product engineering</div><h1>TerrOps turns environmental signals into executable field plans.</h1><p>A GeoDjango/PostGIS platform for organizations managing many outdoor sites: weather ingestion, site intelligence, predicted workload, multi-crew planning, road routing, field evidence, and operational closeout.</p></header>
    <div class="case-summary"><div><span>Stack</span><strong>Django · PostGIS · Celery</strong></div><div><span>Spatial</span><strong>OSRM · VROOM · Leaflet</strong></div><div><span>Data</span><strong>ECCC · GOES · GRIB2</strong></div></div>
    <section class="case-section two-col"><div><div class="section-label">01 / System</div><h2>Environmental data is useful only if it changes tomorrow's plan.</h2></div><div><p>TerrOps ingests public weather and satellite products into site-level environmental facts, combines them with customer sites and operational obligations, and turns the result into explainable rolling plans and field workflows.</p><p>The architecture separates shared site intelligence, planning/execution, and routing artifacts so domain responsibilities remain explicit as the product grows.</p></div></section>
    <section class="case-section"><div class="section-label">02 / Current capabilities</div><div class="capability-grid"><div><h3>Environmental ingestion</h3><p>ECCC HRDPS/HRDPA products, GRIB processing, AMQP-triggered pipelines, GOES radiation observations, and per-site environmental atoms.</p></div><div><h3>Geospatial operations</h3><p>Tenant-scoped Sites, Yards, Site Groups, PostGIS geometry, map-assisted data entry, and site-level intelligence.</p></div><div><h3>Planning + routing</h3><p>Multi-crew Dynamic Day Plans with deterministic and solver-backed planning boundaries, OSRM route geometry, and VROOM/OSRM validation.</p></div><div><h3>Execution evidence</h3><p>Route-independent service completion, BEFORE/AFTER evidence, current service state, and a clean path from planning to field closeout.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section tech-strip"><span>Python 3.12</span><span>Django</span><span>PostgreSQL / PostGIS</span><span>Celery</span><span>RabbitMQ</span><span>Docker Compose</span><span>Ruff</span><span>MyPy</span><span>GitHub Actions</span></section>
    <section class="case-section closing-note"><div><div class="section-label">Product thesis</div><h2>Predict demand early enough to make operations better.</h2></div><p>The goal is not weather visualization. It is an auditable loop: predicted demand → rolling plan → dispatch → field execution → proof → better future planning.</p></section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
